WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.430
An array keeps its items side by side.

2
00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:07.210
To insert at the front

3
00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:10.420
every item has to move over, one by one.

4
00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:13.160
For a list of a million items

5
00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:14.840
that is a million moves

6
00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:17.330
just to add one name at the beginning.

7
00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:19.769
Is there a way to add at the front

8
00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:21.760
without moving anything?

9
00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:23.290
There is

10
00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:26.170
and it works by giving up something we had

11
00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:28.860
the items no longer sit side by side.

12
00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:30.542
In this video we'll see

13
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:32.560
what a linked list is

14
00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:35.120
how to change it by rewiring references

15
00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:37.650
and what it is actually fast at.

16
00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:39.840
A linked list is built from

17
00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:41.800
small boxes called nodes.

18
00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:43.940
Each node holds one value

19
00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:45.080
and one reference

20
00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:46.790
an arrow to the next node.

21
00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:49.590
The nodes can be anywhere in memory.

22
00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:52.380
What keeps them in order is the arrows.

23
00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:55.780
The last node has no next node.

24
00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:58.810
We draw that as a small empty mark.

25
00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:01.675
The list itself is just a reference

26
00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:02.980
to the first node.

27
00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:04.190
We call it head.

28
00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:07.390
We also keep a reference to the last node

29
00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:08.350
called tail.

30
00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:10.060
We'll see why in a moment.

31
00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:12.121
If someone hands us the head

32
00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:14.029
we can reach every item by

33
00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:15.340
following the arrows.

34
00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:17.780
If nobody holds a reference to a node

35
00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:19.330
no one can ever reach it.

36
00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:22.060
Watch how we reach the third item.

37
00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:24.840
We start at head, and follow one arrow

38
00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:26.030
then another.

39
00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:31.083
To reach item i, we have to follow i arrows.

40
00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:33.072
There is no formula that

41
00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:34.250
jumps straight there

42
00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:35.788
like the one for arrays.

43
00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:38.358
Reaching an item by its index takes

44
00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:40.740
time proportional to i.

45
00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:42.610
Now the front.

46
00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:44.920
We want to add A before B.

47
00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:48.640
First, we create a new node holding A.

48
00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:49.880
It's floating free

49
00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:51.470
not yet part of the list.

50
00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:52.710
Step one

51
00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:56.420
make A point to the current first node, B.

52
00:01:56.770 --> 00:02:00.370
Step two: move head to point at A.

53
00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:02.470
Two references changed.

54
00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:04.089
Not one item moved

55
00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:06.509
and it doesn't matter whether the list

56
00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:09.680
has three items or three million.

57
00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:12.830
But the order of those two steps matters.

58
00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:14.590
Let's try them the other way round

59
00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:15.740
on a copy.

60
00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:18.220
Move head to A first.

61
00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:19.720
Now head points at A

62
00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:22.050
and A points to nothing yet.

63
00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:26.010
The old first node, B, still points to C.

64
00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:29.370
But nothing points to B any more: not head

65
00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:30.150
not A.

66
00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:34.200
And no one can reach B, C or D.

67
00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.370
The whole list is lost.

68
00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:41.438
So the rule is: connect the new node to the

69
00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:43.570
rest of the list first

70
00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:46.320
then change what the list starts with.

71
00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:49.530
At the other end, that's what tail is for.

72
00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:51.690
To add E at the end

73
00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:54.170
we make the last node point to E

74
00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:56.180
and move tail to E.

75
00:02:56.530 --> 00:02:58.640
Two reference changes again.

76
00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:00.255
Without a tail reference

77
00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:01.930
we'd have to walk the whole list

78
00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:03.690
first to find the last node.

79
00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:06.320
To remove the first item

80
00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:08.630
we move head to the second node.

81
00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:12.210
The old first node is no longer reachable.

82
00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:13.690
In most languages

83
00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:16.700
the system notices and reclaims its memory.

84
00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:18.530
One reference changed.

85
00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:20.380
More generally

86
00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:23.380
suppose we already hold a node, say B

87
00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:26.550
and we want to insert X right after it.

88
00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:29.010
Same recipe, same order.

89
00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:29.770
First

90
00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:33.150
make X point to whatever B pointed to.

91
00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:36.100
Then make B point to X.

92
00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:39.620
Two references, and nothing else moves.

93
00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.090
But notice the words

94
00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:44.532
a node you already hold.

95
00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:45.995
How do we get hold of the

96
00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:47.480
node in the middle?

97
00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:49.940
We have to walk there from head

98
00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:51.560
one arrow at a time.

99
00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:54.080
For the middle of a list of n items

100
00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:56.310
that's about n over two arrows.

101
00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:58.852
So inserting in the middle of a linked

102
00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:01.310
list is not constant time, either.

103
00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:03.790
The insertion itself is cheap.

104
00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:06.100
The search is not.

105
00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:08.480
Here is the comparison.

106
00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:12.720
Reading item i: the array is constant time

107
00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:15.150
the linked list needs i hops.

108
00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:17.370
Inserting at the front

109
00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:19.610
the array shifts everything

110
00:04:19.610 --> 00:04:22.380
the linked list changes two references.

111
00:04:22.730 --> 00:04:26.060
Inserting after a node we already hold

112
00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:29.300
the array shifts the items after it

113
00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:32.210
the linked list changes two references.

114
00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:35.510
Inserting at a given position, by index

115
00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:38.170
both are proportional to n

116
00:04:38.170 --> 00:04:41.840
one by shifting items, the other by walking.

117
00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:44.080
Neither wins everywhere.

118
00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:46.800
The linked list wins at the front

119
00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:49.670
and wherever we already hold the node.

120
00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:51.300
To sum up.

121
00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:54.920
A linked list is nodes joined by references

122
00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:57.044
and the list is a reference

123
00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:58.890
to the first node.

124
00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:01.110
Insert and remove at the front

125
00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:03.530
and insert after a held node

126
00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:05.610
change only one or two references.

127
00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:08.186
But the order of those changes matters

128
00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:09.428
and getting to a node in the

129
00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:11.320
middle takes a walk.

130
00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:12.540
Next

131
00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:14.780
we'll make the end operations cheap too

132
00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:17.430
with two more kinds of linked list.

133
00:05:18.057 --> 00:05:21.217
Let's build a singly linked list in Java.

134
00:05:21.797 --> 00:05:25.147
A node is a small nested class.

135
00:05:25.147 --> 00:05:26.867
E is a type parameter

136
00:05:26.867 --> 00:05:29.147
the list can hold any type of value.

137
00:05:29.147 --> 00:05:32.020
The value, and the reference to the next

138
00:05:32.067 --> 00:05:33.327
node of the same kind.

139
00:05:33.327 --> 00:05:35.207
When there is no next node

140
00:05:35.207 --> 00:05:36.477
the reference is null.

141
00:05:36.827 --> 00:05:37.877
In Java

142
00:05:37.877 --> 00:05:40.437
a variable of class type holds a reference

143
00:05:40.437 --> 00:05:42.110
never the object itself

144
00:05:42.177 --> 00:05:44.858
so a node pointing at another node

145
00:05:44.937 --> 00:05:47.067
needs no special syntax.

146
00:05:47.647 --> 00:05:51.657
The list keeps head, tail and the size.

147
00:05:51.657 --> 00:05:54.487
For an empty list, head and tail are null.

148
00:05:55.067 --> 00:05:56.537
Here's addFirst.

149
00:05:56.537 --> 00:05:59.677
The new Node gets the old head as its next

150
00:05:59.677 --> 00:06:01.797
and only then is head assigned.

151
00:06:01.797 --> 00:06:03.467
Same order as before.

152
00:06:03.817 --> 00:06:06.327
If tail was null, the list was empty

153
00:06:06.327 --> 00:06:08.887
and the new node is the tail too.

154
00:06:08.887 --> 00:06:10.817
Then size goes up.

155
00:06:11.167 --> 00:06:13.454
No loop, so the cost does not depend

156
00:06:13.517 --> 00:06:14.967
on the length of the list.

157
00:06:15.547 --> 00:06:17.623
removeFirst throws an exception

158
00:06:17.677 --> 00:06:19.197
if the list is empty.

159
00:06:19.197 --> 00:06:21.677
Otherwise it moves head to the next node

160
00:06:21.677 --> 00:06:23.757
and if that leaves the list empty

161
00:06:23.757 --> 00:06:24.987
sets tail to null.

162
00:06:25.337 --> 00:06:26.927
It returns the value.

163
00:06:26.927 --> 00:06:29.007
Nothing refers to the old node

164
00:06:29.007 --> 00:06:31.497
so the garbage collector reclaims it.

165
00:06:32.077 --> 00:06:35.567
insertAfter takes a node we already hold.

166
00:06:35.567 --> 00:06:39.507
The new Node gets node.next as its next

167
00:06:39.507 --> 00:06:42.597
then it is assigned to node.next.

168
00:06:42.947 --> 00:06:46.177
If the node was the tail, the tail moves.

169
00:06:46.177 --> 00:06:48.157
We compare with double equals

170
00:06:48.157 --> 00:06:50.597
which for objects compares references

171
00:06:50.597 --> 00:06:51.597
not values.

172
00:06:51.597 --> 00:06:54.077
That is exactly the question we want

173
00:06:54.077 --> 00:06:55.747
is it the same node?

174
00:06:56.327 --> 00:07:00.077
nodeAt walks from head, following next

175
00:07:00.077 --> 00:07:01.487
index times.

176
00:07:01.837 --> 00:07:04.454
That is the slow part of any

177
00:07:04.547 --> 00:07:06.477
position-based operation.

178
00:07:07.057 --> 00:07:11.207
We build B, C, D, add A at the front

179
00:07:11.207 --> 00:07:15.177
insert X after B, and remove the first item.

180
00:07:15.527 --> 00:07:21.477
The result: B, X, C, D, head B, tail D

181
00:07:21.477 --> 00:07:22.207
size four.

182
00:07:22.787 --> 00:07:24.197
Now the stopwatch

183
00:07:24.197 --> 00:07:27.048
after a warm-up for the virtual machine.

184
00:07:27.117 --> 00:07:28.951
Inserting at the front of the list takes

185
00:07:28.997 --> 00:07:31.517
about a hundredth of a microsecond

186
00:07:31.517 --> 00:07:33.187
at every size.

187
00:07:33.537 --> 00:07:36.604
The array, at sixteen thousand items

188
00:07:36.687 --> 00:07:38.813
takes about one and a half microseconds

189
00:07:38.867 --> 00:07:40.027
for the same insert

190
00:07:40.027 --> 00:07:42.937
and the time doubles when the size doubles.

191
00:07:43.287 --> 00:07:45.497
In the middle, something interesting.

192
00:07:45.557 --> 00:07:48.381
The array needs under one microsecond

193
00:07:48.457 --> 00:07:50.257
at sixteen thousand items.

194
00:07:50.257 --> 00:07:52.717
The linked list needs about six

195
00:07:52.717 --> 00:07:54.687
and the time grows with n.

196
00:07:55.037 --> 00:07:56.539
Both grow with n.

197
00:07:56.627 --> 00:07:58.850
But shifting an array is just

198
00:07:58.927 --> 00:08:00.627
copying neighbouring memory

199
00:08:00.627 --> 00:08:03.164
which the hardware does extremely well.

200
00:08:03.227 --> 00:08:04.911
Following references from node

201
00:08:04.967 --> 00:08:07.387
to node is slower per step.

202
00:08:07.387 --> 00:08:08.767
So in Java

203
00:08:08.767 --> 00:08:10.447
the linked list wins at the front

204
00:08:10.447 --> 00:08:12.437
and the array wins in the middle.

205
00:08:13.017 --> 00:08:13.862
In Java

206
00:08:13.967 --> 00:08:16.226
a linked list is a generic node class

207
00:08:16.287 --> 00:08:18.687
and a few careful assignments.

208
00:08:18.687 --> 00:08:21.287
Next: lists with a second link

209
00:08:21.287 --> 00:08:23.257
and lists that go round in a circle.

